I think the best way to look at it is to see sliming as a disease akin
to the zombie disease from movies like Dawn of the Dead. The slime
cells attach to you, then use your body material to produce a new
green slime. It's their reproduction method. You die, you get
assimilated, there is no "you" left to worry about amulets or
demigod(dess)hood or anything else but oozing around, bumping into
things, and assimilating them.

When you polymorph into a slime, you are never dead - your entire
being is magically converted into a green slime, with your "real"
self's existence-pattern temporarily hidden. When the magic wears off,
your former characteristics reassert themselves, but they were never
gone - they always coexisted with your slime-characteristics, in
whatever potential reality the magic brought into being when it
rewrote your pattern into that of a slime.

So if you get eaten, you're dead - your body was broken down into its
component stuffs to feed green goo, and the fact that it "arose" as
more goo has nothing to do with you any more than mold growing on your
corpse. If you polymorph, you *temporarily changed reality* - for that
duration, you are *supposed* to be a green slime, and your body and
mind are safe in some realm of potentials and ideas until you change
back.

Pets are a strange case, and if I were the game designer I would think
it a bug that slimed pets don't become hostile. If this is working as
intended, then perhaps the magical bond between the pet and its master
- created either through friendship or sorcery - is bound in the game-
world by the pet's raw matter. Then, eating the pet would carry
magical-symbolic significance, which is negligible in most cases but
in the case of green slime, where almost all of the slime-matter is
made from the pet, it deeply affects the slime's being.

I don't know if that made -any- sense, but I tried.

